## Redesign target (flagged by the acceptance test)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` asserts the new required behavior:

```java
/** global-id: Redesign owner identity. The customerCode must become '<REGION>-<HASH8>' where REGION is t... */
...
assertTrue(n.get("customerCode").asText().matches("[A-Z0-9]+-[0-9A-F]{8}")); // <REGION>-<HASH8>
```

So `customerCode` must change from the current `<CITY3>-<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (sequence-based, `CustomerCodeGenerator.format`) to `<REGION>-<HASH8>`:
- REGION = the locality/region for the owner (from `LocalityResolver.resolve(city, postcode)` — item 5/6), matching regex `[A-Z0-9]+` (includes the `UNKNOWN` fallback).
- HASH8 = first 8 UPPER-case hex chars of a SHA-256 (pattern `[0-9A-F]{8}`), i.e. reuse the SHA-256 hex approach in `HouseholdIdGenerator.shaHex` / test helper `shaHex(s, 8)`.

Implications for a change: `CustomerCodeGenerator.format` signature and its call site in `ClinicServiceImpl.createOwner` (lines 279-280) would change; the per-city `sequence` (`countByCity`) would no longer feed the code. Downstream, `MembershipNumberFormatter` and `LuhnCheckDigit` both consume `customerCode` verbatim, so they will keep working but produce different outputs (the Luhn check digit runs over whatever digits appear in the new hash-based code). The test helper `shaHex(String, int)` in `AcceptanceBase` (8 upper-hex chars) shows the exact hashing convention to match.

